  "worked_solution": "We start by integrating the acceleration to get the velocity $v(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{1}{{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{t}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $C,$ we use the fact that $v(1)=0.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0 & =-\\frac{1}{1}+C \\\\ C & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the velocity is given by $v(t)=-\\frac{1}{t}+1.$\n\nNow, we integrate the velocity to get $x(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & =\\int v(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int (-\\frac{1}{t}+1)\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{ln}\u2061t+t+K\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $K,$ we use the fact that $x(1)=4.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4 & =-\\operatorname{ln}\u2061(1)+1+K \\\\ 4 & =-0+1+K \\\\ K & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the position $x$ of the particle at time $t$ is \n\n$$\nx(t)=-\\operatorname{ln}\u2061t+t+3.\n$$",
